// threading.mn — Thread + SharedMemory effects over the `><`/`<|` fanout.
//
// Threading is an effect: no thread keyword, no runtime layer. `Thread`
// declares spawn/join/identity; `SharedMemory` declares the atomic
// instruction surface; the schedule handlers (`parallel_compose` /
// `sequential_compose`) give the ONE fanout verb its execution strategy,
// read live from the `~>` chain at lower where the fanout stands, and
// reaching a callee's fanout by DEMAND through a direct call — the callee
// emitted as a twin under the caller's schedule (SYNTAX §`><`). `<|` and
// `><` are both parallelism — the structural difference is input ownership
// (`<|` ref-borrows one shared input; `><` own-consumes independent
// inputs), never serial-vs-parallel. Output tuple order is source order
// regardless of completion order — determinism is the verb's.
//
// ThreadHandle — the spawn's task record, banked whole. Under
// parallel_compose the payload is the record the substrate allocated at
// spawn ([task closure][completion][result] — $spawn_task_impl in the
// emit preamble); join_task waits on the completion word and reads the
// result through it. Under sequential_compose the payload is the index
// into the handler's results state. Opaque to consumers; `own` to the
// spawn site.
type ThreadHandle = ThreadHandle(Int)

// Thread — spawn/join/identity. Both spawn and join are OneShot: forking
// a continuation across resumes is a different topology (race), not
// Thread. current_id answers 0 on the root instance and the host thread
// id inside a spawned one — a per-instance fact ($tid_g, stamped by the
// thread entry), which is what makes it answerable at all.
effect Thread {
  spawn(() -> a) -> ThreadHandle
  thread_join(ThreadHandle) -> a
  current_id() -> Int
}

// SharedMemory — the wasm atomic instruction set on the shared image.
// Every op is a direct instruction (lower's is_wasi_import_op): no
// handler arm exists or is needed — the name IS the op identity.
// `!SharedMemory` proves no cross-instance synchronization; `Pure +
// !SharedMemory` is the strictest parallelizable-no-sync contract.
effect SharedMemory {
  atomic_load_i32(Int) -> Int
  atomic_store_i32(Int, Int)
  atomic_rmw(Int, RmwOp, Int) -> Int
  atomic_cmpxchg(Int, Int, Int) -> Int
  wait_i32(Int, Int, Int) -> WaitResult
  notify(Int, Int) -> Int
  fence()
}

// RmwOp — the single-operand read-modify-write opcodes, an ADT whose
// declaration order IS the dispatch table ($atomic_rmw_impl br_tables on
// the nullary-ctor tag: RmwAdd=0 … RmwXchg=5). cmpxchg is NOT here — it
// takes two operands (expected, replacement), which the one-operand rmw
// shape cannot express; it is its own op, atomic_cmpxchg.
type RmwOp = RmwAdd | RmwSub | RmwAnd | RmwOr | RmwXor | RmwXchg

// WaitResult — wait_i32's outcome. The declaration order IS the
// memory.atomic.wait32 ABI (0 = notified, 1 = expected mismatch at wait
// time, 2 = timeout): a nullary ctor is its tag word, so the
// instruction's result IS the value — reorder this and the surface lies.
type WaitResult = WOk | WNotEqual | WTimedOut

// WasiThreads — the substrate crossing, three direct-instruction ops
// (is_wasi_import_op; no handler exists). spawn_task allocates the task
// record through the ONE allocator, hands it to the host's thread-spawn
// import, and refuses loudly (trap) on a spawn failure; the module's
// exported thread entry runs the task on the fresh instance, stores the
// result, and releases the completion word. join_task atomic-waits on
// the completion word, then reads the result. thread_id reads the
// per-instance identity the entry stamped. The result crosses as its
// word face (a wide value crosses by reference into the shared image —
// the spawning module's allocator is the shared cell, so every record a
// task allocates is readable from every instance).
effect WasiThreads {
  spawn_task(() -> a) -> Int
  join_task(Int) -> a
  thread_id() -> Int
}

// parallel_compose — the `~> Thread`-class schedule: each spawn is a real
// host thread over the shared image. Stateless — the task record IS the
// spawn's whole state, banked in the ThreadHandle the caller owns (a
// handler-side ledger of handles would be a write-only re-derivation of
// what the caller already carries).
handler parallel_compose {
  spawn(task) => resume(ThreadHandle(spawn_task(task))),
  thread_join(handle) => match handle {
    ThreadHandle(record) => resume(join_task(record)),
  },
  current_id() => resume(thread_id()),
}

// sequential_compose — the same Thread surface at degree 1: spawn runs
// the task inline and banks the result; join reads it back by index.
// The results state is load-bearing here (spawn writes, join reads) —
// the sequential twin of the task record.
handler sequential_compose with results = [] {
  spawn(task) => {
    // drift-audit: ignore — task() is effectful; result feeds the with-clause's push, not chain-position
    let result = task()
    let handle = ThreadHandle(len(results))
    resume(handle) with results = push(results, result)
  },
  thread_join(handle) => match handle {
    ThreadHandle(idx) => resume(list_index(results, idx)),
  },
  current_id() => resume(0),
}

// THE SEQUENCE FANOUT UNDER A THREAD-CLASS SCHEDULE. `fanout(f, xs)` is
// `><` over a runtime sequence (SYNTAX §`><`): each application an
// independent branch, the results collected in order. Under a `Thread`
// install it runs as this fn — every element's branch spawned through the
// installed handler, then every handle joined in source order — reached
// by the lowering exactly as a `><` under the same install is: the site's
// form for the schedule, never a name the program writes. Under no
// install a sequence fanout IS `map`. The loops here are `map`'s own,
// which is sequential by construction — a fanout never fans its own
// bookkeeping.
fn fanout_threaded(f, xs) = xs |> map(spawn_branch(f)) |> map(thread_join)

fn spawn_branch(f, x) = spawn(() => f(x))

// ─── Connection to the kernel ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Handler IS the backend: the same source runs single-threaded or
// multi-core depending only on the installed schedule handler. The
// fanout verb stays pure topology; `lower_fanout_schedule()` reads the
// strategy live from the install chain, and the Threaded arm lowers to
// spawn-all-then-join-all in source order over the strategy-neutral
// branch thunks.
//
// The task record is the unified heap record at the SPACE axis (PLAN
// §5.U): the branch thunk is a contiguous handle-addressed closure, so
// crossing an instance boundary is passing one word — the same record
// that persists by memcpy on the TIME axis. handler = state = closure =
// task: one shape, no parallel thread-record table, no tid ledger.
//
// What this substrate refuses: concurrency keywords (threading IS the
// effect); a num_cores op (preview1 exposes no processor count — an op
// with no truth source is a fabrication, and the spawn degree is the
// fanout's own branch count); shared-mutable-state-by-default
// (SharedMemory is opt-in via the row; `!SharedMemory` is proof of its
// absence); string-keyed rmw dispatch (RmwOp is the ADT and its decl
// order the table).

